<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Black Sea Cossack Host</h1><p class="st-meta">1787 CE – 1860 CE · capital Yekaterinodar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Cossack army formed from former Zaporozhians, which settled the Kuban frontier and founded the city now called Krasnodar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1787, on the eve of war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin">Grigory Potemkin</a> gathered former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/host-of-loyal-zaporozhians">Host of Loyal Zaporozhians</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydir-bilyi">Sydir Bilyi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakhary-chepiha">Zakhary Chepiha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-holovaty">Anton Holovaty</a>, they fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakiv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izmail">Izmail</a>, and in 1790 they were renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Host.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> granted the Host the lands of the Taman peninsula and the right bank of the Kuban, and in 1793 they founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Yekaterinodar</a> as their capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> kept elected officers and a Zaporozhian tradition, farming and guarding a fortified line against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> across the Kuban.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Host fought throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasian-war">Caucasian War</a>. In 1860 it was merged with part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-line-cossack-host">Caucasus Line Cossack Host</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kuban-cossack-host">Kuban Cossack Host</a>. Its Ukrainian heritage shaped the speech and culture of the Kuban region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/black-sea-cossack-host.jpg" alt="Black Sea Cossack Host" width="320" height="429"><figcaption>Image: Korneev E. M. (1782 – 1839) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narody_Rossii_(1812-13,_Paris)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1787 CE – 1860 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yekaterinodar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sydir Bilyi, Zakhary Chepiha, Anton Holovaty</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chernomorian Cossacks</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=black-sea-cossack-host" aria-label="Black Sea Cossack Host on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5637 219 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Black Sea Cossack Host lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5818 759h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5957 475h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5957 475h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=black-sea-cossack-host">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
